,"contentType":"text","elements_assets":[],"style":{"type":"basic default inverted legacy","attached":false,"color":"primary"},"numbering":"A","shuffle":false,"maxSelections":4,"enableRetry":true,"useProjectDefault":true,"showFeedback":true,"spaceTextLikeElements":false,"titleTag":"h4","disablePadding":false,"enableFeedback":true,"variable_name":"","attempts":"1","fullSize":true,"disableClaroPadding":true,"feedback":{"correct":"<div style=\"text-align: center\">A, B, C and D are correct. A - Fairness in predictive models must take precautions to prevent bias from having a discriminatory effect. B - Accountability must be addressed as predictive models are not responsible in the same morally relevant sense as humans. C - Safety requires a model be accurate and reliable since failure can lead to harmful outcomes and undermine public trust. D - Transparency involves the ability to know how and why a model performed the way it did in a specific context and to also understand the rationale behind its behavior.E - Efficiency may be a desired trait of a predictive model but is not a principle applicable to the ethical use of predictive models discussed in this section.<\/div>","incorrect":"<div style=\"text-align: center\">Sorry that is not correct. A, B, C and D are correct. A - Fairness in predictive models must take precautions to prevent bias from having a discriminatory effect. B - Accountability must be addressed as predictive models are not responsible in the same morally relevant sense as humans. C - Safety requires a model be accurate and reliable since failure can lead to harmful outcomes and undermine public trust. D - Transparency involves the ability to know how and why a model performed the way it did in a specific context and to also understand the rationale behind its behavior.E - Efficiency may be a desired trait of a predictive model, but is not a principle applicable to the ethical use of predictive models discussed in this section.<\/div>","partial":""}